, B6? No, because I don't have B6. Wait, maybe there's a miscalculation here. Let me recheck.\n\nThe user says the valid actions are [Chi B8; Pass]. So the system thinks it's possible. How? Let me think again. The discarded tile is B8. To form a Chi, I need two tiles in my hand that can combine with B8 to make a sequence. Let's see: \n\nIf I have B4 and B5, and the discarded is B6, then I can form B4, B5, B6. But the discarded is B8. So possible sequences could be B6, B7, B8 or B7, B8, B9. Do I have any of these? \n\nI have B4, B5, B8, B9. If I have B7 and B9, then B7, B8, B9. But I don't have B7. If I have B6 and B7, then B6, B7, B8. But I don't have B6 or B7. So how can I form a Chi with B8? Maybe the system is considering a different suit? No, B is Dots. \n\nWait, maybe there's a mistake in the hand tiles. The user lists B4:1, B5:1, B8:1, B9:1. So four B tiles. If the discarded tile is B8, then maybe I have B7 and B9 in my hand? Wait, no. The hand tiles as listed don't include B7. Unless there's a typo. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the Chi is for a different suit. But the action is Chi B8, so it's B suit. \n\nHmm. Maybe the system is allowing a Chi even if the sequence isn't possible? Or perhaps I have another tile. Let me check again. \n\nMy B tiles: B4, B5, B8, B9. The discarded tile is B8. To form a Chi, I need two tiles that can form a sequence with B8. For example, B6 and B7 (to form B6, B7, B8), but I don't have those. Or B7 and B9 (to form B7, B8, B9), but I don't have B7. \n\nWait, unless I have B8 in my hand and the discarded B8 allows me to form a pair, but that's a Pung, not a Chi. \n\nThis is confusing. The valid action is Chi B8, but I don't see how that's possible. Maybe there's an error in the problem setup? Or perhaps I'm missing something. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the Chi is for a different sequence. For example, if I have B4, B5, and the discarded B8, maybe I can form B5, B6, B7? No, because I don't have B6 or B7. \n\nWait, maybe the system is consi