, T7, T8], so if I have T7, maybe they already have T6 and T8, so I can't form a T6-T7-T8 chow. So maybe T7 is safe to discard?\n\nB9 is in my hand. The previous player has a meld of [B4, B5, B6], and the next player has [B5, B5, B5]. B9 is still available (unshown B9 is 1), so maybe B9 is a terminal tile. If I discard B9, it's a terminal, which others might want for their hands. But if I keep it, maybe it's part of a pair or a pung. But I only have one B9. So discarding B9 might be a good idea if it's not part of a needed set.\n\nLooking at the J1s. I have two J1s. If I can form a Pung of J1s, that's a 2-point pattern. But I only have two, so if another J1 is available, I could Peng. But in the game history, others have played J3, J2, etc. The unshown J1 is 2, so there are two left. If I discard a J1, maybe someone else can Peng, but if I keep it, maybe I can form a pair or pung later.\n\nSimilarly, J2 is in my hand. Unshown J2 is 2, so two left. Maybe keeping J2 could help with a pair or pung.\n\nLooking at the W tiles. I have W1, W3, W4, W7, W9. The meld is W1-W2-W3. So W1 is already in a meld. In my hand, I have W1, W3, W4. If I discard W3 or W4, others might need them for their chows. For example, the player across has a meld of [W5, W6, W7], so they might want W4 or W8. But W7 is in my hand. The next player has a meld of [W5, W6, W7], so they have W7. Wait, the next player's melds include [W5, W6, W7], but I have W7 in my hand. If I discard W7, they might have already taken it. But since they have a meld of W5-W6-W7, maybe they already have one W7. So discarding W7 might be safe? Or maybe not, because they might need another one for a kong.\n\nBut considering my own hand, I have W1, W3, W4, W7, W9. If I can form any melds with these? W3 and W4 could be part of a W3-W4-W5 chow if I get a W5. But W5 is unshown as 1, so one left. If I discard W4, someone might take it for a chow. Alternatively, if I keep W4, maybe I can form a chow later if I get W5.\n\nBut since